+\section*{Comparison with vanilla Sync HotStuff}
+The baseline here is the standard-synchronous protocol in Figures 1--2 of
+Sync HotStuff~\cite{sync}, with $n=2f+1$ and $f+1$-vote QCs. Both protocols
+forward proposals, multicast votes, and start the nonblocking $2\Delta$
+commit timer at the local vote. The following differences concern leader
+rotation and failure recovery; the diagrams do not change the V4 rules.
+
+\begin{center}\small
+\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{.18\linewidth}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{.32\linewidth}Y@{}}
+\toprule
+Aspect & Vanilla standard synchrony & V4\\\midrule
+Normal leader tenure & A leader continues until failure-driven replacement. &
+After $K$ fresh batches, the terminal QC enables the next scheduled leader.\\
+View identity & One view index $v$. & One composite view $(e,r)$;
+normal rotation increments $r$, recovery increments $e$.\\
+QC versus commit & QC enables a child; the local timer supplies commit. &
+Same timing; a terminal QC additionally enables a different producer
+(Figures~\ref{fig:normal-pipeline}--\ref{fig:normal-rotation}).\\
+First child after normal rotation & No terminal-quota handoff in the baseline. &
+May use the preceding term's terminal QC as its parent certificate.\\
+Progress accounting & Missing proposals are counted by triggered votes. &
+Known valid proposals include certified catch-up; terminal QC disables the
+completed term's missing-progress listener.\\
+Quit scope & Quit the current view and cancel its timers. & Freeze the entire
+current epoch and cancel all its unfinished timers; completed commits remain.\\
+Recovery decision & After the exit wait, report a lock; the next leader
+collects statuses and chooses an anchor. & Agree on a common report vector;
+minimum BLAME selects the term, highest valid QC selects the anchor.\\
+Recovery anchor & Re-certify the chosen anchor without a commit timer. &
+Commit the agreed safe anchor at the recovery decision; no re-certification round.\\
+Recovery waits & Exit wait $\Delta$, leader wait $2\Delta$, then message
+exchanges. & Snapshot wait $\Delta$, then $f+1$ authenticated-broadcast rounds
+of $2\Delta$; sender instances run in parallel.\\
+Old messages & Native view progression and entry locks constrain reuse. &
+New epoch separates signing identities; an old-epoch quit proof cannot
+restart completed recovery.\\\bottomrule
+\end{tabularx}\end{center}
+
+\textbf{What normal rotation saves.} The producer changes at the same QC
+boundary that already enables the next block. In Figure~\ref{fig:normal-rotation},
+Bob does not wait for Alice's final timer. No failure-agreement round is
+paid for a successful handoff. The normal proposal interval and local
+vote-start timer remain those illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:normal-pipeline}.
+This does not make a QC a finality certificate by itself.
+
+\textbf{Why failure recovery changes more.} Normal advancement can put
+replicas in different terms when delayed BLAME certificates appear.
+Independent local minima need not match. V4 first closes a common evidence
+set, then derives both the next view and the anchor from it. The fresh epoch
+prevents a return to Bob from reusing his old signatures. Full-history
+catch-up and epoch-wide evidence handling are also part of the safety proof;
+changing the view counter alone would not establish that proof.
+
+\textbf{Why the recovery anchor is committed.} A malicious successor could
+otherwise repeatedly cancel predecessors' timers while QCs keep advancing.
+Committing the common safe anchor converts that certified progress into
+finality. This step relies on the preservation lemma and the common recovery
+decision, not merely on possession of one QC.
+
+\textbf{The tradeoff.} V4 favors an uninterrupted normal pipeline and pays
+for authenticated agreement on failure. Figure~\ref{fig:recovery-timing}'s
+$8\Delta$ bound for $f=2$ ends at \emph{all recovery decisions}. The baseline's
+$\Delta+2\Delta$ describes only its two waiting stages and excludes message
+exchanges; those numbers are not directly comparable end-to-end latencies.
+Neither communication-optimal recovery nor faster worst-case recovery is
+claimed for V4.
 
